# 6. FUTURE-PROOF INFRASTRUCTURE

The site will likely change in the future.

The following may change:

* main domain
* API domain
* WebSocket domain
* staging domain
* production domain
* CDN
* streaming infrastructure
* API version
* authentication endpoint
* deployment environment

Therefore:

## NEVER hardcode infrastructure URLs throughout components.

Bad:

```text
https://example.com/api/...
wss://example.com/ws/...
http://123.123.123.123:8080/...
```

inside random components.

Instead create a centralized configuration architecture.

Conceptually:

```text
Environment / Runtime Configuration
            ↓
       Configuration Layer
            ↓
       API Client Layer
            ↓
      Feature Service Layer
            ↓
        State Layer
            ↓
      UI / Components
```

All environment-specific values must have a single source of truth.

Examples:

```text
API_BASE_URL
WS_BASE_URL
STREAM_BASE_URL
APP_ENV
```

Use the project's existing environment/configuration mechanism when available.

If the backend domain changes, changing configuration should be sufficient wherever technically possible.

Do not force a developer to search the entire repository for old domains.

