# 58. HOW YOU SHOULD WORK

Use your full reasoning and engineering capability.

Do not rush.

Do not generate random code just to show progress.

Think about dependencies between features before implementing them.

When a feature affects architecture, solve the architecture first.

When a design decision affects usability, evaluate it from the user's perspective.

When a backend limitation affects UX, create graceful degradation.

When a future change is likely, isolate it behind an abstraction.

When existing code is good, preserve it.

When existing code is weak, improve it carefully rather than blindly rewriting everything.

The goal is not to produce the largest amount of code.

The goal is to produce the **highest-quality maintainable production frontend possible**.

